{
 "id": "E72",
 "title": "Do brains get better under a head count? The later brains of four worlds against their earlier ones",
 "thesis": {
  "claim": "Under a coalition counted by heads the brains of a world of equals go on getting better. In the four worlds that Experiment 71 played under the rule from iteration 22,500, the brains of iteration 30,000 beat those of iteration 26,000.",
  "why": "Under the old rule the brains of a world of equals got better for about 2,000 iterations and then stopped (Experiment 54), and a changed brain left as many copies as an unchanged one (Experiment 62). Such a world has no births and no deaths, and a node's new brain is drawn by lot among stakers who all stake alike, so there is little that one brain can do better than another. Under a head count the same worlds lived on with something new: three of the four had times of many births while coalitions still won nearly every node (Experiment 71). A birth is where brains differ in what they leave behind, so selection has something to act on there that it did not have before. If that is so, later brains should beat earlier ones, and also in the second half of the 7,500 iterations, when the brains had 3,500 iterations to get used to the new rule. It may well be otherwise. One world had no births to speak of, and should show a tie. And all brain lines of such a world merge into one within about 500 games by lot alone, so what spreads there may be luck and not a better brain; then the contests are ties, or the later brains are worse, worn by mutations of 30 and 50 numbers.",
  "confirm": "A contest plays 100 iterations in one of the two eras' worlds, under that run's rules, with the brains of the two eras dealt to its agents by coin. A coin is two contests with one deal, the two sides in swapped places; a score is the mean of its coins and its band two standard errors either side. For each of the four worlds of Experiment 71, and each of three pairs of eras, eight coins are played, four in each era's world. The thesis reads one pair: 30,000 against 26,000, pooled over the four worlds, 32 coins. It holds if the later brains' share of the agents has a band that lies wholly above 0.5.",
  "refute": "That band includes 0.5 or lies wholly below it: over those 4,000 iterations under a head count brains did not get better. Reported beside it, with no line drawn: 26,000 against 22,500 and 30,000 against 22,500, where the earlier brains were made under the old rule; each world alone, and the three worlds that had births apart from the one that had none; and the share of the tokens."
 },
